## 3) File-by-File Deep Reverse Engineering

This section explains every file with:
purpose, imports, key classes/functions, how it is called, its inputs/outputs, and failure modes.

I use “Data” to mean an instance of `datasets/simple_data.py:GraphData` (a minimal PyG-like container).

### 3.1 `requirements.txt`

Purpose:
Defines Python dependencies for training, attacks, and ontology exports.

Key packages and why:
`torch`, `torch-geometric` and companions: GNN basics. Note this repo also supports running without PyG by using raw Planetoid loaders and pure PyTorch models.
`deeprobust`: provides reference implementations for Nettack/Metattack/DICE/FGA/GCN surrogate. Several attack modules attempt to use it, with fallbacks when unavailable.
`networkx`, `scipy`: graph metrics and adjacency manipulation.
`sklearn`: metrics and some visualization utilities.
`rdflib`: OWL/RDF/Turtle export. The project is designed to keep core training functional even if rdflib is missing.

Runtime risks:
`deeprobust` may be fragile on newer Python/PyTorch versions.
This repo is running Python `3.13.5`, which is beyond what many ML libraries officially test. The code includes fallbacks to keep the core pipeline working even if `deeprobust` or `torch_geometric` fail.

### 3.2 `.gitignore`

Purpose:
Prevents committing large generated outputs and datasets:
`data/`, `results/`, `checkpoints/`, `*.pt`, and raw dataset downloads.

Important mismatch:
In your working copy, `results/` exists and is modified; git shows changes. That means some results are currently tracked or were added earlier. If you want strict reproducibility, you typically keep results out of git. For a thesis submission, sometimes you intentionally commit key output artifacts. Make an explicit decision.

### 3.3 `datasets/simple_data.py`

Purpose:
Defines `GraphData`, a lightweight container used everywhere so you can run without torch_geometric.

Key pieces:
`DatasetInfo`: stores `num_features`, `num_classes`.
`GraphData` fields: `x`, `y`, `edge_index`, `train_mask`, `val_mask`, `test_mask`, optional `edge_weight`.
`clone()`: deep-copies tensors so attacks and defenses can safely modify without side effects.
`to(device)`: moves tensors to device (CPU/GPU).

Why this matters:
Most code expects PyG-style `data.x`, `data.edge_index`, masks, etc. This class is the “compatibility shim.”

Inputs/outputs:
Input is typically created by dataset loaders (`datasets/planetoid_loader.py`) or dynamic generator.
Output is the same object passed into models, attacks, defenses.

Failure modes:
Some PyG-only utilities assume extra fields; this project avoids that.

### 3.4 `datasets/planetoid_loader.py`

Purpose:
Loads static citation graph datasets: Cora, Citeseer, PubMed.

Two loading modes:
1. Preferred: PyG `Planetoid` with `NormalizeFeatures()`.
2. Fallback: classic Planetoid raw files loader, reading `ind.<name>.*` files.

Key functions:
`load_planetoid(name, root="data") -> (DatasetInfo, GraphData)`
`_planetoid_raw_load(...)`: raw loader logic, including Citeseer isolated node fix.

Inputs:
`name` one of Cora/Citeseer/PubMed.
`root` folder where data is stored (`data/`).

Outputs:
`DatasetInfo` and `GraphData` with `edge_index` (COO from CSR), dense features, integer labels, boolean masks.

Failure modes:
If PyG is not available and raw files are missing, it raises with instructions to run `datasets/download_planetoid_raw.py`.

### 3.5 `datasets/download_planetoid_raw.py`

Purpose:
Downloads raw Planetoid dataset files from the original public repo into `data/<Dataset>/raw`.

Why it exists:
To support environments where `torch_geometric` fails (binary extension mismatch).

Important:
This script uses network access. In restricted environments, it may fail.

### 3.6 `datasets/dynamic_graph.py`

Purpose:
Generates a synthetic evolving graph dataset (dynamic graph snapshots).

Key class:
`DynamicGraphGenerator`
Creates a Barabási-Albert graph, evolves by adding nodes with preferential attachment.

Key method:
`get_pyg_data()` returns a `GraphData` with random features, random labels, and random train/val/test masks.

Scientific meaning:
This is not a standard temporal citation dataset. It is a synthetic “stress test” for robustness over snapshots.
For a publication, reviewers may ask for a real temporal dataset. See Section 10 for extensions.

### 3.7 `models/gcn.py`

Purpose:
Implements a pure PyTorch 2-layer GCN (Kipf & Welling).

Mathematics:
Let `Â = A + I`, `D̂` degree matrix, `S = D̂^{-1/2} Â D̂^{-1/2}`.

Layer 1:
`H^(1) = ReLU(S X W^(0))`

Layer 2:
`Z = softmax(S H^(1) W^(1))`

Implementation details:
`normalized_adjacency(edge_index, num_nodes, edge_weight=None)` builds `S` in scipy CSR.
`_scipy_to_torch_sparse` converts CSR->torch sparse tensor for sparse mm.

Defense integration:
`GCN._get_S(data, layer=1|2)` supports:
`data.edge_weight_l1`, `data.edge_weight_l2` for layer-wise trust weights.
Fallbacks to `data.edge_weight` or unit weights.
This is essential to support the base-paper GNNGuard-style defense and combined defenses.

Debug outputs:
`forward_with_debug(data)` returns:
`pre_aggregation_l1`, `post_normalization_l1`, `post_activation_l1`,
`pre_aggregation_l2`, `post_normalization_l2`,
`softmax_probabilities`, `predictions`,
and a memory estimate.

This is how the project satisfies “print layer-wise outputs.”

Failure modes:
The normalized adjacency is cached inside the `data` object; if edge weights change, the hash is meant to invalidate cache.
If you mutate `data.edge_index` and forget to clear caches, it can reuse stale adjacency. This code attempts to avoid that with a hash.

### 3.8 `models/gat.py`

Purpose:
Implements a pure PyTorch 2-layer GAT (Velickovic et al.) with multi-head attention in layer 1.

Key formulas:
`e_ij^k = a_k^T [W_k h_i || W_k h_j]`
`alpha_ij^k = softmax_j(LeakyReLU(e_ij^k))`
`h_i^(1) = concat_k Σ_{j∈N(i)} alpha_ij^k W_k h_j`
Layer 2 uses a single head producing class logits.

Implementation details:
Uses `scatter_reduce`-based max and sum to compute per-destination softmax.
Ensures self-loops.

Defense integration:
Supports `data.edge_weight_l1`, `data.edge_weight_l2` or `data.edge_weight` and injects it as an additive log prior:
`e = e + log(edge_weight)`.
This is “semantic edge trust” integrated directly into attention.

Debug outputs:
`forward_with_debug` returns attention weights and memory estimates.

Failure modes:
Because attention softmax is computed per-destination, incorrect `dst` indexing or mismatched edge weights can break normalization.
The code checks shape of edge weights.

### 3.9 `experiments/baseline_training.py`

Purpose:
Defines the generic training loop used by `main.py`.

Key functions:
`train_model(model, data, epochs, lr, weight_decay, regularizer=None, reg_weight=0.0, edge_weight=..., edge_weight_l1=..., edge_weight_l2=...)`
`evaluate_model(model, data) -> (metrics_dict, pred_tensor, probs_tensor)`

How it supports defenses:
During training, the loop attaches edge weights to `data` so model uses them.
Regularizer hook supports ontology semantic regularization during poisoning defense retraining.

Loss:
Uses `F.nll_loss(out[train_mask], y[train_mask])` because model outputs `log_softmax`.

### 3.10 `utils/metrics.py`

Purpose:
Computes metrics used in final tables and impact analysis.

Classification metrics:
Accuracy, precision/recall macro, F1 macro, F1 micro.
If probabilities available: ROC-AUC (multi_class='ovr'), log-loss, margin, entropy, confidence.

Robustness metrics:
`ASR` is implemented as relative accuracy drop ratio:
`ASR = (acc_clean - acc_attack) / acc_clean`.
Also computes confidence drop, margin drop.

Graph metrics:
Density, clustering, avg degree, homophily, modularity (label-induced communities), conductance.

Note:
This ASR definition differs from “attack success rate” in targeted attacks (fraction of targets misclassified). This repo uses it as a robustness score.
In your IEEE paper you must define ASR precisely as implemented.

### 3.11 `attacks/poisoning/random_poison.py`

Goal:
Simulate naive poisoning by randomly inserting edges and optionally corrupting features.

Mechanism:
Randomly pick pairs (u, v) and add edges (symmetric).
Optional feature corruption: flip binary entries (0/1) or add small bounded noise.

Impact expectation:
Breaks homophily and injects noisy neighbors into training.
In this repo, poisoning attacks retrain the model on the modified training graph.

Inputs/outputs:
Input `adj` as scipy CSR and optional `features`.
Output `modified_adj` and `modified_features`.

### 3.12 `attacks/poisoning/nettack.py`

Goal (paper-level):
Targeted poisoning attack that reduces classification margin of a target node by modifying edges/features under stealth constraints.

Implementation here:
Tries to use DeepRobust `Nettack` with a surrogate `deeprobust.graph.defense.GCN`.
If DeepRobust unavailable, uses a fallback that flips edges around the target node.

Important:
If DeepRobust works, Nettack is closer to the real paper.
If not, the fallback is only “targeted edge toggle,” which is weaker and less stealthy.

Outputs include proxy values:
`margin_reduction_proxy`, `perturbation_score_proxy`.
These are not the true Nettack scoring function; they are placeholders for reporting.

### 3.13 `attacks/poisoning/metattack.py`

Goal (paper-level):
Meta-learning poisoning attack (bi-level optimization) that optimizes graph perturbations through training dynamics.

Implementation here:
DeepRobust `MetaApprox` path exists but is disabled by default (`use_deeprobust = False`) due to instability on newer builds.
Fallback:
Computes cosine similarity matrix on normalized features.
Scores existing edges by `abs(sim - 0.5)` and flips top-k “most ambiguous” edges.

Scientific implication:
The fallback is not a true bi-level Metattack.
It approximates “global structure degradation” by corrupting edges that are semantically uncertain.
For a publication, you must clearly label this as “Metattack-like proxy” unless you enable and validate DeepRobust.

### 3.14 `attacks/poisoning/dice.py`

Goal:
DICE is a global poisoning attack from DeepRobust that modifies edges based on labels.

Implementation:
Direct DeepRobust call with no fallback.

Risk:
If DeepRobust fails to import, this attack breaks.
In the current `main.py` paper profile, DICE is not included in the static suite; it uses Random/Nettack/Meta and avoids DICE.

### 3.15 `attacks/evasion/structure_evasion.py`

Goal:
Degree-preserving inference-time structural attack around target nodes.

Mechanism:
For each perturbation:
Pick an existing neighbor to remove, pick a non-neighbor to add.
This keeps degree roughly stable, which is a stealth property.

Output:
Returns modified adjacency CSR.

In `main.py`:
This attack is applied to many test nodes (loop), producing a global effect in evaluation even though the mechanism is local per node.

### 3.16 `attacks/evasion/feature_evasion.py`

Goal:
Inference-time feature attack (no training graph modification).

Mechanism:
For each attacked node:
Flip a budget of binary features (0/1).
Add noise to non-binary features.

Important correctness condition:
The code clones data (`attacked = data.clone()`) and writes `attacked.x`, leaving clean training data untouched.
This satisfies the “evasion only modifies test-time inputs” requirement.

### 3.17 `attacks/evasion/fgsm_like.py`

Goal:
FGSM-like feature attack:
`X_adv = X + ε sign(∇_X L)`

Implementation:
Computes loss on test mask, backprop to x, applies sign step, clamps to [0,1].

Impact expectation:
Can be extremely destructive if ε is large, because it pushes many features in the worst direction for the model.
In your current output tables, this attack becomes the most impactful (accuracy ~ 0.002), so the “calibration” logic in `main.py` tries to pick a harmful but still defendable intensity.

### 3.18 `attacks/evasion/adaptive_semantic.py`

Goal:
“Adaptive semantic attack” meant to bypass naive similarity pruning by making feature changes that are semantically plausible but still cause misclassification.

Key idea:
Use ontology artifacts:
feature-to-class affinity matrix,
feature co-occurrence graph,
contradiction pairs.

Mechanism:
Pick a target wrong class (e.g., highest probability class different from true).
Add features highly indicative of target topic, with co-occurrence plausibility.
Optionally remove features indicating true class.
Avoid contradiction pairs if configured.

Research significance:
This strengthens the paper: it shows an attacker aware of your defense assumptions.
You must include the threat model: “adaptive attacker knows ontology constraints.”

### 3.19 `defenses/gnnguard_paper_defense.py`

Purpose:
Implements “base paper” defense, described as GNNGuard-style:
Neighbor importance estimation,
Pruning,
Layer-wise graph memory.

Key formulas implemented:
Edge similarity score from cosine similarity.
Importance: `a_uv = sim(x_u, x_v)^power`.
Pruning: threshold and top-k outgoing edges per node.
Layer-wise memory:
`omega2 = beta * omega1 + (1 - beta) * a2`

Outputs:
Returns `(data_def, w1, w2)` where `w1` and `w2` are edge weights for layer 1 and 2.

Integration:
GCN/GAT consume these via `data.edge_weight_l1` and `data.edge_weight_l2`.

Scope clarification:
This defense changes message passing weights, not the model architecture.

### 3.20 `defenses/feature_defense.py`

Purpose:
Feature smoothing and feature consistency regularization:

Smoothing:
`X_smooth = α X + (1-α) Â X` (implemented via normalized adjacency with self-loops).

Consistency regularizer:
`||X - Â X||^2` (implemented as mean squared residual).

Integration:
`main.py` uses smoothing as part of the base-paper defense calibration (chooses best α on attacked input).

### 3.21 `ontology/ontology_builder.py`

Purpose:
Builds an ontology schema and numeric semantic artifacts that go beyond similarity pruning.

Ontology ≠ similarity pruning (what this file enforces):
Similarity pruning: local geometry only (cosine/Jaccard between two feature vectors).
Ontology defense: typed concepts (topics/subtopics/features), explicit relations (indicatesTopic, contradicts, coOccursWith), constraints (contradiction rules), and inheritance scores. These symbolic structures drive numeric trust and repair signals.

Core outputs: `OntologyArtifacts`

It contains:
`feature_class_affinity` (F,C): feature-to-topic distribution learned from training labels.
`node_topic_confidence` (N,C): topic distribution per node derived from features and affinities.
`feature_cooccur` (F,F): sparse co-occurrence graph between features.
`contradiction_pairs`: feature pairs that should not co-occur (semantic impossibility).
Graph semantics for Cora-like citation graphs:
degree, pagerank (power iteration), local homophily, neighbor entropy, topic mismatch fraction.
Vulnerability scores per node:
LowHomophilyVulnerability, SparseNeighborhoodVulnerability, HighCentralityVulnerability, TopicMismatchVulnerability, BridgeNodeVulnerability.

Why centrality matters:
High-centrality or bridge nodes are more influential in message passing; attacks on them can propagate further.
Ontology builder explicitly computes pagerank and degree, and derives “bridge vulnerability.”

### 3.22 `ontology/ontology_rules.py`

Purpose:
Explicit semantic rule engine:
Computes semantic edge trust, detects contradictions, repairs features, and produces an auditable “rule report.”

SWRLRule:
A minimal representation for documentation/export. These are not executed by a DL reasoner inside Python; they document your semantic logic and can be exported.

Rule categories implemented:
Semantic consistency (topic similarity implies trust).
Contradictory feature detection.
Cross-topic penalty.
Reinforcement rules (feature evidence boosts topic confidence).
Cora-specific planning rules (topic mismatch suspicious edges, homophily collapse triggers purification, embedding drift triggers adversarial training, bridge vulnerability triggers isolation).

Key function:
`semantic_edge_trust(artifacts, edge_index, ...)`:
Trust = cosine(topic_dist_u, topic_dist_v) * conf(u) * conf(v) with penalties for cross-topic mismatch and bridge/mismatch vulnerabilities.

Repair:
`repair_node_features` removes off-topic strong-affinity features and adds on-topic strong-affinity features, bounded by caps.

### 3.23 `ontology/ontology_defense.py`

Purpose:
Implements the ontology-guided defense module that produces:
defended features, optional pruned edges, semantic edge weights, and an optional semantic loss regularizer.

Defense variants (for ablations):
`COOCCURRENCE_ONLY`
`LABEL_AFFINITY_ONLY`
`OWL_RULES_ONLY`
`SEMANTIC_PROJECTION_ONLY`
`FULL_ONTOLOGY`

Core defense actions:
1. Semantic projection:
Reconstruct “semantic features” from node-topic distribution and feature-topic affinity:
`X_sem = P A^T`
Blend:
`X' = (1-λ) X + λ X_sem`
2. Co-occurrence projection:
`X' = (1-λ) X + λ X W` where W is normalized co-occurrence.
3. Rule-based repair:
Detect nodes with contradiction anomaly above threshold and repair features.
4. Semantic edge trust and pruning:
Compute semantic trust and prune edges under threshold; return edge weights.
5. Regularizer (optional during retraining):
Edge consistency term plus topic alignment KL term.

Why this is a “true ontology defense”:
It is driven by typed semantics, contradiction constraints, and rule-based repairs, not just similarity weights.
Numeric matrices are produced from symbolic structures, not from raw geometry alone.

### 3.24 `ontology/ontology_export.py`

Purpose:
Exports ontology artifacts to Protégé-compatible formats using rdflib:
`ontology.owl`, `ontology.rdf`, `ontology.ttl`, and `ontology.swrl` (human-readable rules).

Important detail:
This exporter intentionally keeps SWRL rules as a separate `.swrl` text file unless you extend it to encode swrl:Imp triples.
Protégé will open OWL/RDF/Turtle; SWRL rules can be added/managed directly in Protégé.

Failure mode:
If rdflib is not installed, export is skipped but training/defense continues.

### 3.25 `ontology/csv_to_owl_reasoner.py`

Purpose:
Populates an OWL file for Protégé from experiment CSV.

Inputs:
`results/attack_results.csv` (generated by `main.py`).
`ontology/gnn_attacks_ontology_starter.owl` (starter schema).
Optional `results/ontology_graph_snapshot.json` (Cora semantic individuals: Paper/Topic/CitationEdge/Vulnerability).

Output:
`ontology/gnn_attack_reasoned.owl`

Key point:
Despite its name, this script does not run an OWL reasoner.
It generates a “reasoner-ready” OWL file that you open in Protégé and run HermiT/Pellet there.

What makes it “ontology-rich” (your requirement):
It adds:
Topic individuals,
Paper individuals, each with object properties (hasTopic, hasFeatureVector, hasVulnerability),
FeatureVector individuals,
Vulnerability individuals with data properties (score/type),
CitationEdge individuals with semantic trust metrics and suspicious labels,
DefenseChain and DefenseStage planning individuals for multi-stage defense workflows,
OntologyRule individuals so every defense can be linked to explicit rule semantics.

Scale warning:
The generated OWL can be large. In your current run it is ~12MB. Protégé can handle this but reasoning may be slow.

### 3.26 `defenses/robust_filtering.py`

Purpose:
Contains several classic graph defense utilities:
Jaccard similarity, SVD low-rank purification, similarity-weighted adjacency, top-k pruning, label-guided pruning, robust edge filtering, feature smoothing.

How it is used:
`main.py` uses `svd_defense` as an extra purification stage for poisoning defense in the base-paper defense loop.

Risks:
SVD purification converts adjacency to dense; can be expensive for large graphs.
This is OK for Cora-scale graphs.

### 3.27 `visualization/paper_diagrams.py`

Purpose:
Draws clean “paper-style” block diagrams using matplotlib patches.
Not AI art; it is deterministic vector drawing.

Main exported functions used by `main.py`:
`draw_project_workflow(save_path, attacks, worst_attack, defenses)`
`draw_gcn_layerwise(save_path)`
`draw_gat_layerwise(save_path, heads=4)`
`draw_attack_defense_flow(save_path, worst_attack, defense_names)`

These create exactly the “workflow” and “layer-wise architecture” images you requested.

### 3.28 `visualization/graph_viz.py`

Purpose:
Generates clean vs attacked vs defended graph visual proof, with explicit highlighting:
Attack-added edges (red), attack-removed edges (black dashed),
Defense-added edges (green), defense-removed edges (blue dashed),
Nodes whose features changed (red ring / green ring),
Nodes whose predictions changed (purple ring),
Nodes that became misclassified (black ring).

Key functions:
`visualize_graph_mosaic(...)`: 3-panel single image.
`visualize_triplet_separate(...)`: three separate images with identical layout (important for comparing).
`visualize_attack_suite(...)`: grid showing how each attack changes the dataset.

Scientific interpretation:
These figures are meant to visually validate that:
1) the attack truly changed the dataset,
2) those changes caused node prediction changes,
3) defense changes the dataset again and recovers predictions.

### 3.29 `visualization/plotting.py`

Purpose:
General plots:
confusion matrices, t-SNE cluster plots, layer output heatmaps, robustness curves.

Important stability choice:
`plot_tsne_suite` uses deterministic PCA via SVD by default to avoid platform-specific t-SNE crashes.
It falls back to t-SNE if SVD fails.

### 3.30 `paper_ieee/*`

Purpose:
IEEEtran conference paper scaffold.
It uses figures from `results/` and tables generated from `results/*.csv`.

Key file:
`paper_ieee/main.tex`: paper body.

Key script:
`paper_ieee/scripts/make_tables.py`: converts CSV outputs to LaTeX tables stored under `paper_ieee/tables/`.

Build steps:
Run `python paper_ieee/scripts/make_tables.py`, then compile LaTeX.

Figure alignment issues:
If `\includegraphics` exceeds page margins, fix by using `\linewidth` not `\textwidth`, or using `\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{...}`.
The paper already uses `\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{...}` for some figures; for others, enforce `width=\linewidth` inside `figure` and `figure*`.

### 3.31 `main.py` (the real orchestrator)

Purpose:
This is the end-to-end experiment driver.
It is long because it also contains:
table rendering without tabulate,
attack calibration logic,
defense hyperparameter sweeps,
visualization orchestration,
ontology CSV/OWL exports,
and an explanation generator.

High-level function index (from `rg '^def '`):
Utility: seeding, table rendering, graph conversions.
Attack evaluation: `evaluate_model_under_attacks`.
Defense evaluation: `apply_feature_defenses`.
Dynamic snapshot generation: `save_dynamic_snapshots`.
Ontology export helper: `export_cora_semantic_snapshot`, `export_ontology_artifacts`.
Final pipeline: `main(profile="paper", clean=False, dataset_name="Cora")`.

In Section 4, I do a full execution trace call-by-call.

Known pitfall:
`clean_results_dir()` is currently malformed: its deletion logic appears after a `return` statement from another function due to an indentation/merge error.
So `--clean` may not actually delete old results reliably.

