## 5) Attack Modules: Deep Explanation (Math + Intended Impact)

This section answers: what each attack is supposed to do, why it should affect node classification, and what “impact” means in results.

### 5.1 Poisoning vs evasion (threat model)

Poisoning attack:
Attacker modifies training data.
Your pipeline retrains the model on the poisoned dataset.
Impact appears because model parameters learn wrong neighborhood statistics.

Evasion attack:
Attacker modifies inference-time inputs only.
Your pipeline keeps model parameters fixed; only the forward pass sees modified `(A', X')`.
Impact appears because message passing aggregates corrupted neighbors/features at test time.

### 5.2 Poisoning: Random Structure

Purpose:
Crude baseline: show that random edge insertion + mild feature corruption can degrade performance.

How it changes the dataset:
Adds new edges between random nodes. In citation graphs, these edges violate topic consistency (connect unrelated papers).
Optional: flips a fraction of features globally, simulating text corruption.

Why node classification drops:
GCN/GAT mix features across edges. Random edges inject wrong-topic features into neighborhoods.
Training learns to trust those wrong neighbors, so test predictions degrade.

What you should see in visuals:
Added edges (red) scattered; class clusters become more mixed in embedding plots.

### 5.3 Poisoning: Nettack

Purpose:
Targeted poisoning: degrade classification around specific target nodes by carefully modifying their local structure/features.

In your repo:
If DeepRobust works, it is close to true Nettack.
If not, fallback toggles edges around target nodes.

Expected behavior:
Small number of local changes can flip predictions for targeted nodes.
If you attack many target nodes (as `main.py` does), you get a visible dataset-level accuracy drop.

### 5.4 Poisoning: Meta Attack

Purpose (paper):
Bi-level optimization: choose perturbations that maximize final validation loss after training.

In your repo:
Fallback flips “uncertain similarity” edges.
This tends to degrade homophily and community structure.

Expected impact:
Moderate accuracy drop, especially in GCN, because GCN relies heavily on homophily assumptions.

### 5.5 Evasion: Edge Flip (degree-preserving)

Purpose:
Test-time structural perturbation that changes the neighborhood while keeping degree stable.

How it changes dataset:
For attacked nodes, swap edges: remove one existing neighbor, add a new non-neighbor.

Why it drops accuracy:
The node aggregates from a new neighbor distribution. If new neighbors are off-topic, embedding shifts.

Expected visuals:
Both added and removed edges around attacked nodes; prediction-changed rings should appear.

### 5.6 Evasion: Feature Evasion

Purpose:
Test-time feature corruption to flip predictions without modifying adjacency.

How it changes dataset:
Flips a budget of binary bag-of-words features per attacked node and adds bounded noise to non-binary features.

Why it drops accuracy:
Message passing starts from wrong node features, so both the attacked node and its neighbors receive corrupted signals.

Important:
Training graph is untouched; only inference input changes.

Expected visuals:
Edges unchanged, but many nodes ringed as “feature changed,” and prediction changes.

### 5.7 Evasion: Gradient (FGSM-like)

Purpose:
Maximally adversarial feature attack that uses gradients to move features in the worst direction.

Formula:
`X_adv = clamp(X + ε sign(∇_X Loss), 0, 1)`

Why it can be worst:
It moves features in exactly the direction that increases classification loss, across many nodes at once (test mask in this repo).

Expected behavior:
Strong accuracy collapse if ε is not carefully controlled.
This repo includes “budget calibration” to avoid always choosing an ε that kills accuracy to near 0 (because defenses can’t recover from extreme collapse).

### 5.8 Evasion: Adaptive Semantic

Purpose:
Adaptive attacker that crafts semantically plausible modifications to bypass naive pruning.

What makes it “adaptive”:
Uses ontology artifacts (feature-topic affinity, co-occurrence, contradictions).
Adds features consistent with a target wrong topic, avoids contradictions, and matches co-occurrence patterns.

Expected impact:
Can reduce defense effectiveness because it targets the defense’s assumptions.

---

## 6) Defenses: Deep Explanation (Why They Work, Where They Fail)

You requested:
Three defense types evaluated individually, then combined.
Also: pruning/smoothing/layer-wise memory as in base paper.

### 6.1 Defense type 1: Base paper (GNNGuard-like)

Components:
1) Neighbor importance estimation:
Edge trust from similarity between node representations.
2) Pruning:
Threshold + top-k edges per node.
3) Layer-wise graph memory:
Second-layer weights blend first-layer weights with new similarity.

Why it works:
Adversarial edges are often low similarity; they get down-weighted or pruned.
Memory prevents overreacting to noisy changes layer-to-layer.

Where it fails:
If attacker injects semantically plausible edges (high similarity), pruning may not remove them.
Also, aggressive pruning can delete useful edges and hurt clean accuracy.

### 6.2 Defense type 2: Ontology-only

Components:
Semantic projection pulls features toward a topic-consistent subspace.
Rule-based contradiction repair removes impossible feature combinations.
Semantic edge trust uses topic distributions plus vulnerability penalties, not just raw similarity.

Why it works:
Feature attacks are countered by projection and repair.
Structural attacks are countered by semantic edge trust and pruning of topic-mismatched edges.

Where it fails:
If ontology is weak (no meaningful feature-topic structure), projection becomes “similarity smoothing” and loses novelty.
If attacker is adaptive (Adaptive Semantic Attack), contradictions may not trigger.

### 6.3 Defense type 3: Combined (Base paper + Ontology)

Idea:
Ontology projects and repairs features, producing semantic trust.
Then base-paper neighbor importance + memory stabilizes message passing.
Multiplying weights by semantic trust makes it harder for low-semantic-trust edges to contribute even if they look geometrically similar.

Expected result:
Best recovery on the most impactful attack, especially for feature attacks.

---

## 7) Ontology + SWRL Reasoning: Extremely Deep Explanation

This repo has two distinct “ontology layers.”

Layer A: Numeric ontology (used directly in defense)
`ontology/ontology_builder.py`, `ontology/ontology_rules.py`, `ontology/ontology_defense.py`
These create matrices and constraints and run rule logic in Python.

Layer B: Symbolic OWL population for Protégé
`ontology/gnn_attacks_ontology_starter.owl`
`ontology/csv_to_owl_reasoner.py`
These produce a reasoner-ready OWL file enriched with individuals and data properties from experiments.

Important to understand:
OWL reasoning in Protégé is not currently used to automatically execute defense code inside `main.py`.
Instead:
`main.py` runs defenses directly.
Then it exports results into OWL so you can inspect and reason in Protégé.
This is still publishable as an “explainable semantic backbone,” but the “closed-loop OWL triggers Python defense execution” is a future extension (see Section 10).

### 7.1 What classes/properties exist (starter ontology)

Open `ontology/gnn_attacks_ontology_starter.owl` in Protégé.
Core classes include:
Attack, Defense, Dataset, Model, ImpactMetric, Perturbation, OntologyRule.
Attack subclasses: EvasionAttack, PoisoningAttack, BackdoorAttack, StructuralAttack.
Defense subclasses: SimilarityPruningDefense, NeighborImportanceDefense, LayerMemoryDefense, GraphPurificationDefense, FeatureSmootheningDefense, AdversarialTrainingDefense, SemanticRuleDefense, SubgraphIsolationDefense.
Dataset semantics: Paper, Topic, CitationEdge, FeatureVector, ClassLabel.
Graph topology semantics: Neighborhood, Community, BridgeEdge, SuspiciousEdge, TriggerMotif.
Vulnerability classes: LowHomophilyVulnerability, BridgeNodeVulnerability, HighCentralityVulnerability, TopicMismatchVulnerability, SparseNeighborhoodVulnerability.
Defense chain planning: DefenseChain, DefenseStage, nextDefenseStage.

Object properties (examples):
targetsDataset, affectsModel, mitigatedBy, measuredBy.
hasTopic, hasFeatureVector, hasVulnerability.
citesFrom, citesTo.
recommendedChain, hasDefenseStage, stageDefense, nextDefenseStage.
usesRule (Defense -> OntologyRule).

Datatype properties (examples):
accuracyBefore, accuracyAfterAttack, accuracyAfterDefense, hasSeverityScore.
centrality/homophily fields populated from snapshot JSON.

### 7.2 “Every datapoint has object property + data property + rule” requirement

Your pipeline enforces this in `export_cora_semantic_snapshot`:
Every paper gets at least one vulnerability relation even if weak (picks best).
Every paper gets numeric properties (degree/pagerank/homophily/entropy).
Edges get semantic scores and suspicious labels.
Rules are created as OntologyRule individuals and linked via defense modules.

That means:
In Protégé, each Paper individual has:
Object property links: hasTopic, hasFeatureVector, hasVulnerability.
Datatype properties: degree, pagerank, local_homophily, neighbor_entropy, topic_mismatch_frac, etc.

### 7.3 Why ontology ≠ similarity pruning (the proof argument)

Similarity pruning:
`trust(u,v) = cosine(x_u, x_v)` or Jaccard.
No concept types, no inheritance, no impossibility constraints.
No explanation beyond “vectors are similar.”

Ontology defense in this repo:
Has explicit semantic commitments:
Topic classes and subtopics.
Feature-to-topic affinity built from training labels (interpretable evidence).
Co-occurrence semantics (features that normally appear together).
Contradiction constraints (features that should never appear together).
Vulnerability modeling using graph topology and citation semantics (centrality, bridge risk, homophily collapse).
Rule-based repair (actively editing features to restore semantic consistency).
Semantic edge trust uses topic distributions plus vulnerability penalties, not raw similarity alone.

This is a knowledge-guided robust message passing framework, not a geometric filter.

### 7.4 How SWRL reasoning fits

In Python:
Rules are implemented procedurally in `OntologyRuleEngine`.
This gives deterministic, fast defense behavior.

In Protégé:
The `.owl` file includes individuals and data properties.
You can add SWRL rules in Protégé to infer:
which edges are suspicious,
which nodes are vulnerable,
which defense chain should be recommended.

This repo already creates DefenseChain/DefenseStage individuals for each run based on severity and attack type. This is a “semantic defense planning” artifact.

