## 10) Research Novelty and Publication-Level Improvements

### 10.1 What is already novel in this repo

Ontology-guided semantic defense that is not just similarity pruning:
contradictions, topic hierarchy, vulnerability modeling, semantic projection, semantic edge trust, and repair.

Defense chain planning encoded into OWL:
DefenseChain and DefenseStage individuals created per run.

Adaptive semantic attack:
Attacker aware of semantics, attempting to bypass naive defenses.

### 10.2 Missing novelty that reviewers may ask for

True SOTA baselines:
Your repo contains a “DEFENSE_BENCHMARK.csv,” but you must ensure each baseline corresponds to a real method and is implemented/validated.
If you claim “RobustGCN, GraphDefense, GNNGuard,” implement them faithfully or clearly label approximations.

Real temporal dataset:
Dynamic part is synthetic BA snapshots. Add an actual temporal citation dataset for a stronger claim.

Closed-loop OWL->Python execution:
Right now, OWL is populated after the experiments. Next step is to let OWL reasoning trigger which defenses to execute automatically.

### 10.3 Three IEEE-level improvements

1) Replace Metattack fallback with a verified Metattack implementation (DeepRobust) or a modern PyTorch-native implementation and validate against known benchmarks.
2) Add targeted-ASR definition: for targeted attacks, report fraction of targeted nodes misclassified, in addition to relative accuracy drop.
3) Add ablation of vulnerability semantics: show accuracy recovery when centrality/homophily rules are removed, to prove why graph semantics matter.

### 10.4 Three PhD-level extensions

1) Learning thresholds/rules:
Instead of fixed SWRL thresholds (homophily < 0.45), learn thresholds from data using Bayesian calibration or meta-learning.
2) Ontology-driven defense executor:
Run an OWL reasoner and automatically schedule defense chains (prune -> memory -> purify -> retrain) based on inferred vulnerabilities.
3) Knowledge graphs beyond dataset-derived ontology:
Integrate external scientific ontologies or topic models (LDA/BERT embeddings) to ground the “Topic” concept beyond labels.

---

## 11) Study Guide Mode (Viva + Teaching Script)

### 11.1 If you must explain in 60 seconds

“We trained 2-layer GCN and GAT on Cora-like citation graphs for node classification. We implemented poisoning and evasion attacks that perturb adjacency or features. We measured robustness via accuracy/F1/ROC-AUC and impact via accuracy drop. We then defended the worst attack using three defense types: a base-paper GNNGuard-style pruning and layer-memory method, an ontology-guided semantic defense that repairs features and reweights edges based on topic consistency and contradiction rules, and a combined defense. We generated final tables, graph-diff visuals, embedding cluster plots, and an OWL ontology populated from experiments for Protégé-based reasoning and explainability.”

### 11.2 Common viva questions with answers (starter set)

Q: What is the difference between GCN and GAT?
A: GCN uses fixed normalized adjacency weights. GAT learns attention weights per edge, allowing the model to choose which neighbors matter more.

Q: Why do attacks work on GNNs?
A: Because message passing amplifies small input perturbations across neighborhoods and layers.

Q: Poisoning vs evasion?
A: Poisoning changes training data so learned parameters are corrupted. Evasion changes only inference inputs; parameters stay fixed.

Q: Why is “ontology” not just similarity?
A: Similarity compares vectors. Ontology encodes typed concepts (topics/subtopics), constraints (contradictory features), inheritance, vulnerabilities (centrality/homophily), and rule-based repairs, producing interpretable trust and repair signals.

Q: What does the ontology OWL file do?
A: It provides a Protégé-compatible symbolic representation of attacks/defenses/metrics and dataset semantics, allowing reasoners and SWRL rules to infer vulnerabilities and recommended defense chains.

---

## 12) Known Pitfalls, Bugs, and How to Defend Your Work

1) `clean_results_dir` bug:
The deletion logic appears after a `return` in another function due to indentation issues, which can make `--clean` unreliable.
If asked in viva: explain you intended to delete old results and can fix by moving deletion code inside `clean_results_dir`.

2) “Meta attack” is a fallback approximation:
Be explicit in writing: “Metattack-like proxy” unless DeepRobust is enabled and verified.

3) DeepRobust compatibility:
On Python 3.13, some DeepRobust components may fail.
Your repo mitigates by providing fallbacks and avoiding DICE in the main static suite.

4) ASR definition:
This repo uses relative accuracy drop ratio as ASR.
If asked: define it precisely, and optionally add a targeted-ASR metric for targeted attacks.

---

## 13) What to Read Next (If You Are Studying This)

1) Start with `results/EXPLANATION.md` for a beginner explanation of outputs.
2) Then read `models/gcn.py` and `models/gat.py` with `forward_with_debug`.
3) Then read `main.py` in this order:
Dataset load -> baseline train -> payload building -> evaluate attacks -> pick worst -> defenses -> tables -> OWL export -> visuals.
4) Then read ontology:
`ontology/ontology_builder.py` -> `ontology/ontology_rules.py` -> `ontology/ontology_defense.py` -> `ontology/csv_to_owl_reasoner.py`.

