# APPENDIX B: `main.py` Annotated Walkthrough (Block-by-Block)

This is the closest useful “line-by-line” explanation for a 2,300-line orchestrator.
If you want literal line numbers, run:

```bash
nl -ba main.py | less
```

## B.1 Imports section

`main.py` imports:
Dataset loaders (`load_planetoid`, `DynamicGraphGenerator`).
Training loop (`train_model`, `evaluate_model`).
Models (`GCN`, `GAT`).
Attacks (random poisoning, nettack, metattack, structure evasion, feature evasion, FGSM-like, adaptive semantic).
Defenses (feature smoothing, filtering, GNNGuard paper defense, ontology defense).
Ontology export (`generate_reasoned_ontology`, `OntologyBuilder`, `OntologyGuidedDefense`).
Metrics (`compute_robustness_metrics`, `perturbation_rate`, `compute_graph_metrics`).
Visualization functions.

Design intent:
`main.py` is a single “reproducible experiment runner” that creates all tables/figures/OWL artifacts for the paper in one run.

## B.2 Seeding and deterministic setup

`set_seed(42)` sets Python random, numpy, and torch seeds.
Matplotlib is set to Agg backend and a safe MPLCONFIGDIR is configured to avoid permissions issues.

## B.3 `clean_results_dir()`

Intent:
Delete everything under `results/` on demand.

Actual state:
The function currently contains only a docstring.
The deletion implementation appears later in the file but is accidentally placed after `export_cora_semantic_snapshot` and after a `return`, making it unreachable.

Effect:
`python3 main.py --clean` may not actually clear old results.

This matters scientifically:
If old outputs remain, you can confuse “latest vs stale” tables/figures.

## B.4 Dataset load + baseline training

Loads dataset: `load_planetoid(dataset_name, root="data")`.
Builds `adj_clean` from `edge_index`.
Trains:
`GCN(num_features, 16, num_classes)` for 120 epochs.
`GAT(num_features, 8, num_classes, heads=4)` for 120 epochs.
Evaluates both and prints metric dicts.

## B.5 Ontology build

Creates `OntologyGuidedDefense(dataset_name=..., export_dir="results/ontologies", export_owl=True)`.
Calls `fit(data, class_names=cora_topics)` to build artifacts on clean training graph.
Exports additional CSV artifacts under `results/ontologies/<Dataset>/`.

## B.6 Attack payload construction

Builds a list of payload dicts for 6+ attacks, each with:
`name`, `type` ("poison" or "evasion"), budgets list, and `make_data(budget)` to produce attacked GraphData.

Important:
Nettack and edge-flip attacks are applied to multiple nodes, so their effect is visible at dataset-level accuracy.

## B.7 Attack evaluation (per model)

Calls:
`evaluate_model_under_attacks("GCN", gcn, gcn_builder, data, payloads_gcn)`
and similarly for GAT.

For poisoning: trains fresh model on attacked data for each candidate budget.
For evasion: evaluates baseline model on attacked data.

Budget calibration:
Picks a budget that produces a measurable accuracy drop so you can demonstrate defenses.

## B.8 Worst attack selection

Computes accuracy drop relative to baseline and selects max drop attack.
Prints:
`The most impactful Attack is : ...`

## B.9 Defense evaluation

Calls `apply_feature_defenses` once for GCN and once for GAT on the same “worst attack” dataset.
This returns:
rows of defense candidates,
best base-paper defense,
best ontology defense,
best combined defense.

Then the code appends these to the main dataframe and constructs:
Pre-defense table: baseline + all attacks.
Post-defense table: baseline + worst attack + 3 defense winners.

## B.10 Terminal output and CSV persistence

Prints exactly two terminal tables (pre and post).
Writes:
`results/FINAL_TABLE_PRE_DEFENSE.csv`
`results/FINAL_TABLE_POST_DEFENSE.csv`
`results/metrics_terminal.txt` (terminal capture).

## B.11 OWL population

Builds `results/attack_results.csv` (compact).
Exports `results/ontology_graph_snapshot.json` (dataset semantics).
Calls `generate_reasoned_ontology(...)` producing `ontology/gnn_attack_reasoned.owl`.

## B.12 Figures

Graph diff:
If worst attack is structural, edges differ; if feature-only, node rings show changed features and prediction changes.

Class clusters:
Uses embeddings from `gcn.get_embeddings(...)` and optionally defended embeddings.

Workflow and architecture diagrams:
Uses `visualization/paper_diagrams.py`.

## B.13 Dynamic dataset snapshots

Generates 4 snapshots and stores them as `.pt` files under `data/dynamic/`.
Also exports dynamic graph metrics CSV if possible.

---

