# APPENDIX D: Metrics Deep Dive (Definitions, Where Computed, How to Explain)

This appendix answers:
What every column means, where it is computed in code, and how to explain it scientifically.

## D.1 Where metrics are computed in code

Classification metrics live in `utils/metrics.py:compute_classification_metrics`.
Robustness metrics live in `utils/metrics.py:compute_robustness_metrics`.
Rows are assembled in `main.py:make_result_row`.
Worst attack is selected by `main.py` using `Accuracy Drop`.

## D.2 Column-by-column definitions (FINAL_TABLE_*.csv)

`Model`
GCN or GAT.

`Attack`
String label for baseline, each attack, and defense methods (defense entries contain “Defense:” in the name).

`Accuracy`
Test-set accuracy: fraction of correct predictions on `data.test_mask`.
Computed by sklearn `accuracy_score` inside `compute_classification_metrics`.

`Precision`, `Recall`
Macro-averaged precision/recall (unweighted mean across classes).

`F1`, `Macro F1`, `Micro F1`
Macro F1 is class-balanced, micro F1 behaves like accuracy in multiclass.
In your tables, `F1` duplicates macro F1 (the code writes both).

`ROC-AUC`
Multiclass one-vs-rest ROC-AUC (`roc_auc_score(..., multi_class='ovr')`) if probability matrix is available.

`Log-loss`
Cross-entropy on the test set computed from predicted probabilities.
If model becomes confidently wrong, log-loss rises sharply.

`Classification Margin`
Implemented as mean(top1_prob - top2_prob) across test nodes.
Interpreted as how “separated” the decision is; attacks reduce separation.

`Robustness Score`
Defined as `max(0, 1 - ASR)` where ASR is defined below.

`Attack Success Rate`
In this repo, ASR is defined as relative accuracy drop:
`ASR = (acc_clean - acc_attack) / acc_clean`.
This is not the same as targeted-ASR used in some attack papers.
In a paper/viva, you must define ASR exactly as above.

`Margin Drop`
Difference in average margin: `margin_clean - margin_attack`.

`Perturbation Rate`
Fraction of entries changed.
If adjacency: `(A != A') / (N*N)` using sparse nnz difference.
If features: `(X != X') / (N*F)` using elementwise difference.
Computed by `utils/metrics.py:perturbation_rate`.

`Perturbation Budget`
The “dial” for attack strength:
edges flipped/added for structure attacks,
flips per node for feature attacks,
epsilon for FGSM-like attacks,
and so on. In `main.py` these budgets are normalized to a single numeric.

`Accuracy Drop`
Computed as: `Accuracy(Baseline) - Accuracy(Attack)`.
This is the impact metric used to choose the worst attack.

## D.3 How to interpret a result scientifically (template)

Use this template in your report/paper:

1) Threat model statement:
“We evaluate poisoning attacks (train-time perturbations with retraining) and evasion attacks (test-time perturbations without retraining).”

2) Quantitative claim:
“Attack X reduces GCN test accuracy from A to A-Δ and macro-F1 from F to F-ΔF, with perturbation rate r under budget b.”

3) Mechanistic explanation:
“The perturbation changes neighborhood mixing (message passing), producing embedding drift that increases class overlap, observed in the class cluster figure.”

4) Defense claim:
“Defense Y recovers accuracy by pruning low-trust edges, smoothing high-frequency feature noise, and repairing semantic contradictions; the recovered graph shows higher homophily and modularity.”

## D.4 Important nuance in your current run (why some defenses do not improve)

Your `results/metrics_terminal.txt` shows the worst attack is `Evasion: Gradient (FGSM-like)` with accuracy ~ 0.002 for GCN.
This is an “extreme collapse regime.”

In this regime:
Pruning-based structural defenses cannot help much because adjacency is unchanged (feature attack).
GNNGuard-style neighbor trust on representations can fail because:
the representations themselves are corrupted by adversarial features.

Ontology-only defense improves to ~0.101, but base-paper and combined remain near 0.002 in this run.
This should be presented as:
“FGSM-like global feature corruption is too strong for similarity/memory defenses, but semantic projection recovers partial performance.”

If you need the narrative “attack hurts, defense recovers clearly,” you must constrain ε and/or attack scope so the attack remains in a defendable range.
This is a scientific tuning problem, not a code bug.

